\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Most teams in the 
\f1\b Harmonization of MODIS and VIIRS Hot Spots
\f0\b0  track make a fatal mistake on day one: they download CSVs from NASA FIRMS, concatenate them by 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 latitude
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 longitude
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , and 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 acq_date
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , and plot fire counts over time.\
\
If you do that, your historical baseline is broken. Because VIIRS has a 
\f1\b ~7\'d7 smaller pixel area
\f0\b0  than MODIS and spans three active satellites (Suomi NPP, NOAA-20, NOAA-21) compared to MODIS's two (Terra, Aqua), naive merging produces an artificial jump in fire counts and Fire Radiative Power (FRP) after 2012\'962017 that looks like a climate spike\'97when it is actually just sensor physics.\
\
To harmonize both datasets with scientific precision, solve the structural differences between the two sensors before building your burn calendars or anomaly dashboards.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

