\f1\b \cf2 2. Scan-Angle & "Bow-Tie" Deduplication\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Both sensors suffer from the 
\f1\b bow-tie effect
\f0\b0  away from nadir (high View Zenith Angles), where consecutive scan lines overlap on the ground and record the exact same fire twice within seconds. \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fix:
\f0\b0  Never treat FIRMS coordinates as dimensionless points. Use the 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 scan
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  and 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 track
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  columns (actual pixel dimensions in km) to calculate pixel footprint area ($A = \\text\{scan\} \\times \\text\{track\}$). Within a single satellite overpass (
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 < 2 minutes
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 ), cluster detections whose footprints overlap by greater than 50% (or centers within $1.5 \\times \\max(\\text\{scan\}, \\text\{track\})$), keeping the near-nadir observation (smallest footprint area) or averaging their FRP rather than summing duplicates. \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 3. Footprint-Aware Spatial Grid Aggregation\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do not compare raw hotspot point counts between MODIS and VIIRS. Reproject both datasets onto a common 
\f1\b equal-area discrete global grid
\f0\b0 \'97such as 
\f1\b Uber H3 hexagons
\f0\b0  (Resolution 7 at ~5.16 km\'b2 for regional/national dashboards, or Resolution 8 at ~0.74 km\'b2 for local fire perimeters) or a 1 km \'d7 1 km equal-area grid.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Assign each hotspot to all grid cells intersected by its actual 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 scan \'d7 track
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  footprint (area-weighted allocation), rather than dropping the entire fire into a single center-point cell.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How to aggregate metrics per cell:
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl1
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 FRP (Megawatts):
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Sum
\f0\b0  VIIRS FRP within a 1 km reference cell. Because FRP is a physical measure of radiant energy release, the sum of sub-pixel VIIRS detections physically corresponds to the integrated 1 km MODIS pixel FRP.\uc0\u8232 \
\ls3\ilvl1
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fire Extent / Activity:
\f0\b0  Track 
\f1\b active burned cell count
\f0\b0  (binary presence per cell) and 
\f1\b sub-pixel active fraction
\f0\b0  (fraction of the 1 km cell covered by 375 m VIIRS pixels) rather than raw point counts.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 4. Overpass-Window Clustering (Eliminating Constellation Inflation)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Because Aqua, Suomi NPP, NOAA-20, and NOAA-21 all cross the equator in the early afternoon (~1:30 PM local solar time, separated by ~50 minutes), a single wildfire is routinely imaged 3 to 4 times in one afternoon.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fix:
\f0\b0  Bucket detections into 
\f1\b diurnal overpass windows
\f0\b0  (e.g., Morning 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 08:00\'9612:00
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , Afternoon 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 12:00\'9617:00
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2 , Night 
\fs30 \cf3 \cb4 \strokec3 20:00\'9604:00
\fs24 \cf2 \cb1 \strokec2  local solar time).\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Within the same grid cell and overpass window, 
\f1\b do not sum FRP across different satellites
\f0\b0 . Instead, apply a 
\f1\b quality-prioritized fusion rule
\f0\b0 : take the FRP from the observation with the smallest viewing angle (closest to nadir, where VIIRS geometric fidelity is highest), falling back to MODIS only when VIIRS has no coverage or cloud gaps.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 5. Dual-Tier Cross-Calibration for Multi-Year Anomaly Detection\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b0 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Space Apps prompt explicitly emphasizes 
\f1\b historical comparisons and anomalies against multi-year averages
\f0\b0  alongside 
\f1\b emergency manager monitoring
\f0\b0 . A single harmonized series cannot optimize both without bias unless you calibrate sensitivity: \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl1
\f1\b \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tier A \'97 Long-Term Climate & Anomaly Baseline (2000\'962026):
\f0\b0  During the sensor overlap years (2012\'96present), fit an empirical 
\f1\b View Zenith Angle (VZA) correction
\f0\b0  to MODIS off-nadir FRP and apply 
\f1\b quantile mapping
\f0\b0  (or Reduced Major Axis regression) by biome between aggregated VIIRS and MODIS FRP. Apply a minimum FRP threshold (~8\'9610 MW) to VIIRS when computing 20-year historical anomalies so small agricultural burns detected only by VIIRS do not create false positive anomalies against 2000\'962011 MODIS baselines. \uc0\u8232 \
\ls5\ilvl1
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	
\f3 \uc0\u9702 
\f1 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Tier B \'97 High-Sensitivity Operational Layer (2012\'962026):
\f0\b0  Retain un-thresholded 375 m VIIRS detections fused with VZA-corrected MODIS gap-fills for real-time fire front tracking, early ignition alerts, and burn-calendar granularity.\uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

